  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 210 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe day started off mostly cloudy, which slightly slowed heating,\nbut skies cleared allowing temperatures to climb into the lower \nto mid 80s. ACARS soundings show 200-500 SBCAPE J/kg with little \nto no CIN. Precipitable water values are around an inch. We seem \non track for scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms later\nthis afternoon and evening. Main threat will be brief heavy rain,\nsmall hail, and wind gusts up to 40 mph. Can't rule out a stray \nsevere thunderstorm or two across the eastern plains where better \ninstability resides. The showers and thunderstorms will progress \neastward and end by mid-evening over eastern Colorado.\u00a0\n \nThe upper level low over the Great Basin slowly moves eastward \ntonight and Saturday, increasing QG ascent over the region. Cross \nsections show skies should be mostly clear to partly cloudy \ntomorrow morning, allowing temperatures to reach their convective \ntemperature (mid to upper 70s). Scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms are expected again, but they should begin a couple \nof hours earlier. Storms increase in coverage and intensity as \nthey move onto the plains during the late afternoon and early \nevening. MLCAPE reaches 500-1500 J/kg and 0-6km bulk shear is \n30-40 knots. A few of the storms could become severe, mainly over \nthe plains where the instability will be better. Models agree we \nwill see a good amount of convection tomorrow, but they disagree \nby a few hours on the timing. Will it be an early to late \nafternoon event, or a mid afternoon to early evening one?\n\nThe upper level low will move east\u00a0across Wyoming on Sunday. At \nthe surface east winds will bring cooler temperatures and keep low\nlevel moisture in place. We will likely see low clouds and if \nthey are thick enough, there will be areas of drizzle Sunday \nmorning. Highs are only expected to reach the mid 60s to lower \n70s. Even with this, the cold air aloft and moist low levels \nyield\u00a0SBCAPE up to 1000J/kg. If temperatures can climb enough to \nbreak the cap, scattered thunderstorms are expected. Given the \ninstability and good shear, a few severe storms will be possible. \nHistorically, days like this favor the Palmer Divide where \nbreaking the cap will be easier. Because of the cold air aloft, \nhail will be the main threat from the thunderstorms.\u00a0\n\nOn Monday, flow aloft returns to the southwest as the upper level\nlow shifts east of the region. A shortwave ridge embedded in the \nsouthwest flow is expected to bring a mild and mostly dry day. \nHowever the 12Z ECMWF slowed the eastward progression of the low \nand shows showers lingering into Monday morning over eastern \nColorado. \u00a0 For Tuesday through Friday, the models loosely agree, \nbut details\u00a0vary. It begins Tuesday when the closed low over \nsouthern California weakens and starts to lift northeast across \nthe Great Basin. Models vary on the strengthen, timing, and track \nof this feature Tuesday and Wednesday, which sets up the pattern \nfor the rest of the week.\u00a0 Ridging will take over the \nintermountain west and plains mid week. Does this upper level \ntrough get trapped underneath the ridge (GFS solution) or does it \nquickly lift northeast into the Northern Plains (ECMWF solution)? \nWe'll see a chance for showers and thunderstorms Monday night and \nTuesday as the upper-level feature embedded in the southwest flow \nlifts northeast across the region. If the slower solutions are \ncorrect, the chance for showers could linger into Wednesday. For \nThursday and Friday, ridging is expected over the region. It could\nend up being a \"dirty ridge\" with subtropical moisture trapped \nunderneath, continuing the chance for showers and thunderstorms. \nTemperatures from Tuesday onward are expected to be near to \nslightly above normal.",
